				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-c238084 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-446be41 " data-id="c238084" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="c238084">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-c29e219 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="c29e219" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-c3bce29 elementor-widget elementor-widget-image" data-id="c3bce29" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="image.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
																<a href="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/329ec795-283a-4996-b9ee-f3bda5fd791d.png" target="_blank">
							<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="768" height="432" src="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/329ec795-283a-4996-b9ee-f3bda5fd791d-768x432.png" class="attachment-medium_large size-medium_large wp-image-3993" alt="" srcset="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/329ec795-283a-4996-b9ee-f3bda5fd791d-768x432.png 768w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/329ec795-283a-4996-b9ee-f3bda5fd791d-300x169.png 300w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/329ec795-283a-4996-b9ee-f3bda5fd791d-1024x576.png 1024w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/329ec795-283a-4996-b9ee-f3bda5fd791d-1536x864.png 1536w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/329ec795-283a-4996-b9ee-f3bda5fd791d.png 1672w" sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 768px" />								</a>
															</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-7d86919 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-e331b4d " data-id="7d86919" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="7d86919">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-31963ce e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="31963ce" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-10ec7d0 elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor" data-id="10ec7d0" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="text-editor.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
									<p>We’re disappointed that Worthing Borough Council has decided against taking our proposal for the West Building Shelter forward after the first round. We put a great deal of thought into what the shelter could become and how it could benefit the town.</p><p>As sixth-generation Worthing residents, we wanted to create a place that felt rooted in the town where we grew up. Pebble Beach Café would have given local artists a place to show their work and supported local businesses by sourcing food, drink and services close to home.</p><p>We felt the shelter deserved a beautiful design that would sit comfortably on Worthing’s seafront. We proposed a welcoming café that made the most of its coastal setting and gave residents and visitors somewhere to meet and enjoy the sea views.</p><p>We would have loved to bring that vision to life. Thank you to everyone who helped us put the proposal together and shared their ideas and encouragement along the way.</p><p>We wish the winning tender every success and look forward to its positive impact on our great town.</p>								</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-0517555 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-e663faa " data-id="0517555" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="0517555">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-1082177 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="1082177" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-c510933 elementor-widget elementor-widget-heading" data-id="c510933" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="heading.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
					<h5 class="elementor-heading-title elementor-size-default">12 November 2026 - Planning appeal and tree planting update
</h5>				</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-552a956 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-96b285b " data-id="552a956" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="552a956">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-b1cace7 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="b1cace7" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-1d6d019 elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor" data-id="1d6d019" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="text-editor.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
									<h6><strong>Goring Hall Conservation Area</strong></h6><p>Worthing Borough Council has submitted its questionnaire to the Planning Inspectorate for our Goring Gap appeal. The Council answered “Yes” under “Conservation area” and listed a PDF titled <em>Goring Hall Conservation Area</em>. Click <a href="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Conservation-area-error-submission.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a> to view. The Council’s published maps show that our site at Marine Drive and Amberley Drive lies outside the designated boundary.  We have asked the Council to clarify its answer and to correct the appeal record to the Planning Inspector if the entry was intended to indicate that our site lies within a designated conservation area. <a href="https://www.adur-worthing.gov.uk/media/Media%2C99414%2Csmxx.pdf#page=3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Goring Hall Conservation Area appraisal map</a> · <a href="https://www.adur-worthing.gov.uk/media/Media,99441,smxx.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Worthing conservation areas map</a></p><p>You will see from the Council submission our site is NOT classed as Green Belt land. Click <a href="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Conservation-area-error-submission.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>.</p><h6>The Local Green Gap policy</h6><p>The case officer’s report referred to the “openness” of the Gap. On 28 October 2026, Worthing’s Planning Committee backed the officer’s recommendation to oppose our appeal.</p><p>During the Local Plan examination, Inspector Steven Lee recommended removing “openness” from criterion ii of <strong>Policy SS5 (Local Green Gaps)</strong>. He explained that the designation’s purpose is to maintain the physical and visual separation of settlements. Worthing Borough Council adopted the Local Plan incorporating that change on <strong>28 March 2023</strong>. <a href="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Worthing_Local_Plan_Inspectors_Report_Steven_Lee.pdf#page=19" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Local Plan Inspector’s report, paragraphs 71–72</a> · <a href="https://www.adur-worthing.gov.uk/media/Media%2C169484%2Csmxx.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Local Plan adoption statement</a></p><p>We believe the appeal should be assessed against the adopted wording of Policy SS5, including whether the proposal would undermine the physical or visual separation of Goring and Ferring or compromise the integrity of the Gap.</p><h6>Trees to increase biodiversity and help nesting birds</h6><p data-pm-slice="1 1 []">We’re pleasantly surprised that, as far as we’re aware, only one objection has been lodged to our tree planting application. Eight of the proposed trees would surround the children’s sandpit and provide natural shade as they mature.</p><p>We also propose planting trees along the eastern and northern boundaries to screen the site and provide a natural windbreak. Feedback this summer from customers was the building styles along the Ferring boundary detracted from the view. Once the trees mature we will not be able to see Ferring.</p><p>The tree planting application is separate from our wider planning appeal, which includes the children’s sandpit. The Council has voted to oppose that appeal. If the Inspector dismisses the appeal, the sandpit cannot go ahead under these plans, even if the tree planting can proceed. <a href="https://planning.adur-worthing.gov.uk/online-applications/applicationDetails.do?keyVal=TNUV96CBHL500&amp;activeTab=summary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View the tree planting application</a></p><p>We hope the Inspector will give weight to what the sandpit would offer local families: a shaded place by the sea where children can play safely and families, of all ages, can spend time together.</p>								</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-2954212 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-d047d38 " data-id="2954212" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="2954212">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-1dc539e e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="1dc539e" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-47d346c elementor-widget elementor-widget-heading" data-id="47d346c" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="heading.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
					<h5 class="elementor-heading-title elementor-size-default">10 November 2026 - Drainage and CIL charge</h5>				</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-3c4e0e8 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-4438b49 " data-id="3c4e0e8" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="3c4e0e8">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-87b2179 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="87b2179" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-3b50ef6 elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor" data-id="3b50ef6" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="text-editor.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
									<p data-pm-slice="1 1 []"><strong>Drainage</strong></p><p>WSP, Worthing Borough Council’s drainage consultant, has reviewed our application for a tractor barn at Goring Gap (AWDM/1248/26) and raised no objection on surface water drainage grounds. WSP recommends that the Council request a  drainage plan before construction begins to show how rainwater from the barn would be managed without increasing flood risk. We propose connecting to the existing Southern Water mains drainage system that runs alongside our property, subject to confirmation that it can accept rainwater.  <a href="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Goring-Gap-mainsd-drains-and-sewers_Page_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View the mains drainage plan</a>.</p><p><strong>Community Infrastructure Levy (CIL)</strong></p><p>Worthing Borough Council has calculated the proposed barn’s total floorspace at 115.36 m². Although this exceeds the 100 m² threshold, the amount payable depends on the type of development. The Council’s charging schedule sets a rate of £0 per m² for ‘all other development’. We expect that rate to apply to the proposed tractor barn, subject to the Council’s confirmation. <a href="https://www.adur-worthing.gov.uk/worthing-cil/about-cil/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View Worthing’s CIL charging rates</a>.</p>								</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-5f0cbc7 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-5cb739c " data-id="5f0cbc7" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="5f0cbc7">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-a9e6191 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="a9e6191" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-097d1bb elementor-widget elementor-widget-heading" data-id="097d1bb" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="heading.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
					<h5 class="elementor-heading-title elementor-size-default">09 November 2026 - Solar powered barn and electric tractors will help the enviroment and biodiversity. </h5>				</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-92cc506 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-be828a4 " data-id="92cc506" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="92cc506">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-3442854 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="3442854" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-614bd4f elementor-widget elementor-widget-image" data-id="614bd4f" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="image.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
																<a href="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Goring-Gap-Tractor-Barn.png" target="_blank">
							<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="768" height="512" src="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Goring-Gap-Tractor-Barn-768x512.png" class="attachment-medium_large size-medium_large wp-image-4157" alt="" srcset="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Goring-Gap-Tractor-Barn-768x512.png 768w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Goring-Gap-Tractor-Barn-300x200.png 300w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Goring-Gap-Tractor-Barn-1024x683.png 1024w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Goring-Gap-Tractor-Barn.png 1536w" sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 768px" />								</a>
															</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-0d3492b e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-ab96d38 " data-id="0d3492b" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="0d3492b">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-5e7939e e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="5e7939e" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-5b63d20 elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor" data-id="5b63d20" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="text-editor.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
									<p data-pm-slice="1 1 []">We’d like to explain why we’re proposing a tractor barn at Goring Gap and how we plan to use two electric tractors to maintain the site.</p><p><strong>Why we need a barn</strong></p><p>We currently store our mowing equipment off site. Every time we cut the grass, we have to bring the equipment to Goring Gap and take it away again. This is both time consuming and enviromentally unfriendly. The proposed barn would give us a secure place to store and charge the equipment on site, saving time on each visit. We’re also proposing solar panels on the barn roof as part of our move towards running the site entirely on renewable energy.</p><p><strong>Why we’re proposing two electric tractors</strong></p><p>It currently takes nearly five hours to cut the grass with our petrol ride-on mower. By using two electric tractors fitted with mowing equipment, we aim to bring that down to around 30 minutes. Switching to electric equipment would also reduce our use of fossil fuels, help the enviroment and biodiversity.</p><p><strong>How we plan to fund it</strong></p><p>The land is held through our pension schemes. We’re exploring whether the schemes can fund the barn and whether any tax allowances or grants could help with the cost of the equipment, solar panels or charging facilities. Any support would depend on the eligibility rules and how each purchase is funded.</p>								</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-38b1c73 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-4baf30a " data-id="38b1c73" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="38b1c73">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-b1cb8b9 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="b1cb8b9" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-d4139aa elementor-widget elementor-widget-heading" data-id="d4139aa" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="heading.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
					<h5 class="elementor-heading-title elementor-size-default">05 November 2026 - The Sandpit</h5>				</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-7ca93e3 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-142d3b7 " data-id="7ca93e3" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="7ca93e3">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-7952d72 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="7952d72" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-afc8a9d elementor-widget elementor-widget-image" data-id="afc8a9d" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="image.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
																<a href="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-sandpit.png" target="_blank">
							<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="768" height="384" src="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-sandpit-768x384.png" class="attachment-medium_large size-medium_large wp-image-4434" alt="" srcset="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-sandpit-768x384.png 768w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-sandpit-300x150.png 300w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-sandpit-1024x512.png 1024w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-sandpit-1536x768.png 1536w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-sandpit.png 1774w" sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 768px" />								</a>
															</div>
				</div>
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-01191eb elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor" data-id="01191eb" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="text-editor.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
									<p data-pm-slice="1 1 []">Our <a href="https://planning.adur-worthing.gov.uk/online-applications/applicationDetails.do?keyVal=TGPDTNCBFRC00&amp;activeTab=summary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">planning application</a> includes a large sandpit where children can play safely and families can spend time together and socialise outdoors. Our <a href="https://planning.adur-worthing.gov.uk/online-applications/applicationDetails.do?keyVal=TNUV96CBHL500&amp;activeTab=summary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">proposed tree planting</a> would provide natural shade as the trees mature.</p><p>The wider proposals also include a gentle tumbling mound for toddlers and a boules area. Together, these form part of our vision for Goring Gap.</p><p>The application has attracted objections, and Worthing’s Planning Committee voted unanimously to oppose our <a href="https://www.theargus.co.uk/news/26683799.worthing-councillors-fight-planning-appeal-goring-gap-plans/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">appeal</a>. You can read the report of the committee’s decision below.</p><p>The councillors recorded as attending the meeting were:</p><table><tbody><tr><th>Councillor</th><th>Role</th></tr><tr><td><a href="https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/mgUserInfo.aspx?UID=115">Noel Atkins</a></td><td>Committee Member</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/mgUserInfo.aspx?UID=302">Richard Nowak</a></td><td>Committee Member</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/mgUserInfo.aspx?UID=470">Helen Abrahams</a></td><td>Chair</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/mgUserInfo.aspx?UID=378">Rosey Whorlow</a></td><td>Committee Member</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/mgUserInfo.aspx?UID=647">Jeremy Carter</a></td><td>Committee Member</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/mgUserInfo.aspx?UID=641">Debbie Woudman</a></td><td>Committee Member</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/mgUserInfo.aspx?UID=523">Dom Ford</a></td><td>Substitute Member</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The proposals will now be assessed independently by the Planning Inspector.</p><p>The above AI impression shows how the sandpit could look as the trees mature, with picnic tables nearby where families can sit and watch their children play safely. The above Councillors have all opposed this.</p><p>This is how Goring Gap looked when we took it on (click <a href="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/pictures/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>) and this is length some people have gone to in order to stop the sandpit being made available for children to play in. Click <a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/crk2ey2d67ro" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a> </p><p data-pm-slice="1 1 []">Our beach is a much-loved place, but hazards such as the protruding rusty iron nails, shown here, are a constant reminder of why safe play spaces matter. Parents what to relax by the beach knowing their children can play safely.</p><p>We believe all our proposals would be a positive addition to Worthing and Goring Gap — a place for families, community and nature.</p><p>We now look forward to the independent Planning Inspector’s assessment and decision.</p>								</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-441993b e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-01551cc " data-id="441993b" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="441993b">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-99adcd6 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="99adcd6" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-daadaad elementor-widget elementor-widget-image" data-id="daadaad" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="image.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
																<a href="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/IMG_7002-scaled.jpg" target="_blank">
							<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="768" height="1024" src="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/IMG_7002-768x1024.jpg" class="attachment-medium_large size-medium_large wp-image-4484" alt="" srcset="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/IMG_7002-768x1024.jpg 768w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/IMG_7002-225x300.jpg 225w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/IMG_7002-1152x1536.jpg 1152w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/IMG_7002-1536x2048.jpg 1536w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/IMG_7002-scaled.jpg 1920w" sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 768px" />								</a>
															</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-44c5ade e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-cfc4da3 " data-id="44c5ade" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="44c5ade">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-94767b8 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="94767b8" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-42f3f09 elementor-widget elementor-widget-heading" data-id="42f3f09" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="heading.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
					<h5 class="elementor-heading-title elementor-size-default">04 November 2026 - Planning Appeal update</h5>				</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-44388b2 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-6d07a6d " data-id="44388b2" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="44388b2">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-db98e66 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="db98e66" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-6225f07 elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor" data-id="6225f07" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="text-editor.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
									<p>The Planning Inspectorate has formally confirmed acceptance of our appeal against the non-determination of our planning application for Goring Gap.</p><p>The appeal timetable is:</p><table><tbody><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Deadline</th></tr><tr><td>Local planning authority questionnaire</td><td>11 November 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Local planning authority statement</td><td>9 December 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Interested party comments</td><td>9 December 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Final comments</td><td>23 December 2026</td></tr></tbody></table><p>We will publish further updates as the appeal progresses. Thank you for your continued interest and support.</p>								</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-50a7c75 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-0089805 " data-id="50a7c75" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="50a7c75">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-45c5a04 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="45c5a04" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-fdab61b elementor-widget elementor-widget-heading" data-id="fdab61b" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="heading.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
					<h5 class="elementor-heading-title elementor-size-default">02 November 2026 - Further planning applications</h5>				</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-7066cc4 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-abe6941 " data-id="7066cc4" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="7066cc4">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-c27adb4 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="c27adb4" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-283f344 elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor" data-id="283f344" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="text-editor.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
									<p>We’ve submitted two further applications as part of our plans for Goring Gap as a community green space.</p><p>The first application seeks planning permission for a barn to securely store the tractors and equipment we use to maintain the land.</p><p data-pm-slice="1 1 []">Some residents have asked why we need a barn and suggested that a ride-on mower should be sufficient.</p><p>It currently takes nearly five hours to cut the grass at Goring Gap using our petrol ride-on mower. Our aim is to reduce this to around 30 minutes by using two larger electric tractors fitted with mowing equipment.</p><p>Moving to electric machinery would reduce mowing time and remove petrol use from this part of our maintenance work. The proposed rooftop solar panels would also support charging from renewable energy.</p><p>We intend to make use of any available tax relief and grant funding for which the investment qualifies. The purpose of the barn is straightforward: to provide secure, practical storage for the equipment needed to maintain Goring Gap.</p><p>We hope the Planning Inspectorate will take these practical maintenance needs into account when considering our separate appeal on the wider proposals for Goring Gap.</p><p><a href="https://planning.adur-worthing.gov.uk/online-applications/applicationDetails.do?keyVal=TN8KISCBHFM00&amp;activeTab=summary">View the tractor barn application</a></p><p>The second application seeks a Certificate of Lawful Development to establish whether the proposed tree planting can go ahead without planning permission. The trees would provide shade and shelter and create new habitats for wildlife.</p><p>Our wider proposals, which the Council is opposing at appeal, include a tree-shaded sandpit for children. This separate application asks the Council to confirm whether the tree planting itself can go ahead. </p><p><a href="https://planning.adur-worthing.gov.uk/online-applications/applicationDetails.do?keyVal=TNUV96CBHL500&amp;activeTab=summary">View the tree planting application</a></p>								</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-19d0e3e e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-125e204 " data-id="19d0e3e" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="19d0e3e">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-3a5b4a1 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="3a5b4a1" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-1412f81 elementor-widget elementor-widget-image" data-id="1412f81" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="image.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
																<a href="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-trees-Nov-2026-scaled.jpg" target="_blank">
							<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="768" height="543" src="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-trees-Nov-2026-768x543.jpg" class="attachment-medium_large size-medium_large wp-image-4327" alt="" srcset="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-trees-Nov-2026-768x543.jpg 768w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-trees-Nov-2026-300x212.jpg 300w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-trees-Nov-2026-1024x724.jpg 1024w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-trees-Nov-2026-1536x1086.jpg 1536w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-trees-Nov-2026-2048x1448.jpg 2048w" sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 768px" />								</a>
															</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-1a0972c e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-19290b5 " data-id="1a0972c" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="1a0972c">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-3e26510 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="3e26510" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-8b34ae2 elementor-widget elementor-widget-image" data-id="8b34ae2" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="image.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
																<a href="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-tree-image.png" target="_blank">
							<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="768" height="576" src="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-tree-image-768x576.png" class="attachment-medium_large size-medium_large wp-image-4326" alt="" srcset="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-tree-image-768x576.png 768w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-tree-image-300x225.png 300w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-tree-image-1024x768.png 1024w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Goring-Gap-tree-image.png 1448w" sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 768px" />								</a>
															</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-0a343e2 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-1d0e855 " data-id="0a343e2" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="0a343e2">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-55d8654 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="55d8654" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-7438439 elementor-widget elementor-widget-heading" data-id="7438439" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="heading.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
					<h5 class="elementor-heading-title elementor-size-default">30 October 2026 - Openness is the word! </h5>				</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-b69c4e1 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-873dcb9 " data-id="b69c4e1" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="b69c4e1">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-aebd056 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="aebd056" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-477d7ce elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor" data-id="477d7ce" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="text-editor.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
									<p>As many people will know, we are seeking full planning permission to operate Goring Gap as a community green space, supporting biodiversity, health and wellbeing, and recreation, while promoting local businesses and tourism in Worthing and West Sussex.</p><p>Worthing Borough Council’s Planning Committee has now voted unanimously to oppose these proposals at appeal. Further information is available <a href="https://www.facebook.com/story.php?story_fbid=1132972996354111&amp;id=100089242627417">here</a>. Our appeal against non-determination is now with the independent Planning Inspectorate.</p><p>We remain confident in the merits of our proposals, although we are disappointed that the Council intends to contest the appeal. We have previously challenged the Council’s position successfully in court: our premises licensing appeal was allowed by the Magistrates’ Court, despite the Council defending its decision with a specialist licensing barrister. Our planning appeal will now receive an independent assessment on its own merits.</p><p>We encourage residents to read three documents central to understanding the planning issues:</p><ul data-spread="false"><li><p><a href="https://www.adur-worthing.gov.uk/media/Media,169486,smxx.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The adopted Worthing Local Plan</a>, which sets out the Council’s adopted planning policies.</p></li><li><p><a href="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Worthing_Local_Plan_Inspectors_Report_Steven_Lee.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Local Plan Inspector’s report</a>, which explains the Inspector’s findings and the changes required before adoption.</p></li><li><p><a href="https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/documents/g2381/Public%20reports%20pack%2028th-Oct-2026%2018.30%20Worthing%20Planning%20Committee.pdf?T=10" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Planning Committee report</a>, which sets out the officer’s assessment and recommended reasons for opposing our proposals.</p></li></ul><p>In particular, we invite residents to search these documents for the word <strong>“openness”</strong>. Read what the Independent Inspector said about its use in relation to Policy SS5, compare this with the policy’s adopted wording, and then consider the approach taken in the committee report. The Council are even using the term openness in the press. See <a href="https://www.theargus.co.uk/news/26683799.worthing-councillors-fight-planning-appeal-goring-gap-plans/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>.</p><p>Our concern is that the Council’s assessment relies on a concept that the Planning Inspector required to be removed from the policy. We believe committee members should explain how they reconciled the officer’s reasoning with the final wording of the Local Plan adopted by the Council on 28 March 2023.</p><p>This raises a clear question: <strong>does the Council’s assessment apply the policies as finally adopted, taking account of the changes required by the Planning Inspector?</strong> We will ask the Planning Inspectorate to examine this issue as part of our appeal.</p><p>Our case officer, Gary Peck, is also the Council’s Head of Planning. If the appeal decision identifies a substantive error in the interpretation or application of the adopted policies, we believe the Council should explain how that arose, what steps it will take to address it, and whether the same approach has affected other refusal decisions.</p><p>Residents and applicants should be able to have confidence that planning policies are applied consistently, transparently and in accordance with their adopted wording.</p><p>Both Worthing Borough Council and the Planning Inspectorate have confirmed that the land benefits from permitted development rights allowing temporary use for up to 28 days each calendar year, subject to the applicable restrictions.</p><p>That could mean 14 full weekends, or one day a weekend over 28 weeks—providing opportunities for community events throughout much of the spring and summer, subject to any necessary licences and other requirements.</p><p>Goring Gap therefore already has a lawful route to host a range of activities. We hold a seasonal premises licence covering March to September, permitting alcohol sales and music events within its conditions. The police have also confirmed their acceptance of our car parking arrangements, and we have achieved a Food Hygiene Rating of 5—the highest possible rating.</p><p>Our appeal seeks planning permission for a longer operating season and community facilities, including a children’s sandpit and campsite. Alongside this, our proposed tree planting would provide shade, shelter and habitats for wildlife, supporting biodiversity. Together, these proposals would give the community more regular access to an attractive, welcoming space and allow us to plan with greater certainty.</p><p>Sadly, Worthing Borough Council has voted to oppose our proposals at appeal. The planning merits will now be considered by the independent Planning Inspectorate.</p><p>We remain committed to Goring Gap’s future as a welcoming community green space and tourist destination. We will continue pursuing the permissions needed to deliver that vision.</p>								</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-e811c52 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-cedf2bd " data-id="e811c52" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="e811c52">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-4d71a0d e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="4d71a0d" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-eb04a3e elementor-widget elementor-widget-heading" data-id="eb04a3e" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="heading.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
					<h5 class="elementor-heading-title elementor-size-default">29 October 2026 - Goring Gap planning appeal to proceed following committee vote</h5>				</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-20dab30 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-366d63f " data-id="20dab30" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="20dab30">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-d3ab31e e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="d3ab31e" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-51600f2 elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor" data-id="51600f2" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="text-editor.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
									<p>Worthing Borough Council’s Planning Committee voted unanimously on 28 October to support the Council’s position that the Goring Gap planning application should have been refused. The proposal will now be considered by the Planning Inspectorate through our existing appeal against non-determination.</p><p>We are disappointed by the committee’s position, but remain committed to making the case for a managed coastal leisure and community space at Goring Gap.</p><p>Our proposal seeks to bring together outdoor recreation, facilities for visitors and a sustainable approach to managing this seafront site. During our first season, we achieved a Food Hygiene Rating of 5 and moved to operating entirely on solar power.</p><p>We believe the proposal should be assessed against the Local Plan as a whole, with proper weight given to its community, recreation, visitor-economy and environmental benefits alongside the policies protecting the Goring Gap.</p><p>During the committee discussion, some councillors expressed support for the principle of a campsite but suggested it should be located elsewhere, including on brownfield land.</p><p>Chris Dixon, Director of Goring Gap Ltd, said:</p><p>“We are naturally disappointed by the committee’s decision. We have worked hard to improve the land and create a welcoming place for people to enjoy the coast.</p><p>“For many people, the setting is a central part of a camping or caravanning holiday. Access to the sea, attractive surroundings and opportunities to enjoy the outdoors are precisely what draw visitors to places such as Worthing.</p><p>“Camping and caravanning are enjoyed by many local residents too. We believe Worthing should consider how it can welcome those same visitors and support its own tourism economy, while protecting the qualities that make our coastline special.</p><p>“The question should be whether a carefully managed seasonal campsite can work at Goring Gap, assessed on the evidence and its particular merits. Its coastal setting is an important part of that assessment.</p><p>“We recognise the importance of protecting the Goring Gap. Our case is that carefully managed recreation and community facilities can support that purpose, and we will now present our evidence to the Planning Inspectorate.</p><p>“Thank you to everyone who has visited, supported us and taken the time to share what Goring Gap means to them. Your encouragement has made a real difference.”</p><p>We will review the committee’s published reasons and continue preparing our appeal submissions. Further updates will be published on this website as the appeal progresses.</p><p>Planning application reference: AWDM/0737/26</p>								</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-4b05882 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-df4f134 " data-id="4b05882" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="4b05882">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-93dd943 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="93dd943" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-002412e elementor-widget elementor-widget-heading" data-id="002412e" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="heading.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
					<h5 class="elementor-heading-title elementor-size-default">20 October 2026 - Planning Update</h5>				</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-59b7f19 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-cd71752 " data-id="59b7f19" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="59b7f19">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-b7c0a87 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="b7c0a87" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-26869e0 elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor" data-id="26869e0" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="text-editor.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
									<p data-pm-slice="1 1 []">The Planning Inspectorate has confirmed that a case officer has been assigned to our appeal against Worthing Borough Council’s failure to determine our planning application.</p><p>Following our appeal for non-determination, the Council has now published its officer’s report for the Planning Committee meeting on 28 October. It recommends that the Council contest our appeal on the basis that planning permission would have been refused. The report can be viewed <a href="https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/documents/g2381/Public%20reports%20pack%2028th-Oct-2026%2018.30%20Worthing%20Planning%20Committee.pdf?T=10">here</a>.</p><p>We disagree with the recommendation and have submitted a detailed response, asking that it be circulated to all Committee members. Our response addresses the photographic evidence, the site’s coastal recreation and community benefits, the comparison with Brooklands Park, and the progress made on highways matters.</p><p>We are disappointed by the lack of clear, coordinated communication during the application process. We have largely had to monitor the Planning Portal ourselves to identify requests for information and organise our responses.</p><p>The report itself acknowledges that its policy assessment could have been undertaken earlier, while highways discussions continued. In our view, those fundamental concerns should have been clearly explained much sooner, giving us a meaningful opportunity to respond and explore potential solutions. We do not consider the delay in bringing those concerns forward acceptable.</p><p>The Committee meeting will help establish the Council’s position in the appeal. Given the limited preparation time, we are unlikely to attend, but we have asked that our written response be considered. Any non-attendance should not be interpreted as agreement with the report or its recommendations.</p><p>We remain committed to presenting our case to the Planning Inspectorate for independent determination and will publish further updates as the appeal progresses.</p>								</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-22e1430 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-f8c5102 " data-id="22e1430" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="22e1430">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-3bb95f0 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="3bb95f0" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-5642123 elementor-widget elementor-widget-heading" data-id="5642123" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="heading.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
					<h5 class="elementor-heading-title elementor-size-default">12 October 2026 - Planning Update</h5>				</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-498a459 e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-dfc1978 " data-id="498a459" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="498a459">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-127b52c e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="127b52c" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-51f5151 elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor" data-id="51f5151" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="text-editor.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
									<p data-pm-slice="1 1 []">The Planning Inspectorate will now determin our planning application. Please check back here for updates or the planning portal at Worthing Borough Council &#8211; click <a href="https://planning.adur-worthing.gov.uk/online-applications/applicationDetails.do?keyVal=TGPDTNCBFRC00&amp;activeTab=summary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>. </p>								</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-61e199b e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-f6f8ab5 " data-id="61e199b" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="61e199b">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-f1afd6f e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="f1afd6f" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-b22993f elementor-widget elementor-widget-heading" data-id="b22993f" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="heading.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
					<h5 class="elementor-heading-title elementor-size-default">21 September 2026 — The sun has set. Our first season has now officially ended!</h5>				</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-8d3d60f e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-11e0d3e " data-id="8d3d60f" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="8d3d60f">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-6ab961f e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="6ab961f" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-f078585 elementor-widget elementor-widget-image" data-id="f078585" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="image.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
																<a href="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/IMG_0392-scaled.jpg" target="_blank">
							<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="768" height="576" src="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/IMG_0392-768x576.jpg" class="attachment-medium_large size-medium_large wp-image-4057" alt="" srcset="https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/IMG_0392-768x576.jpg 768w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/IMG_0392-300x225.jpg 300w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/IMG_0392-1024x768.jpg 1024w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/IMG_0392-1536x1152.jpg 1536w, https://www.goringgap.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/IMG_0392-2048x1536.jpg 2048w" sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 768px" />								</a>
															</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-0de1d6d e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-8b4d63a " data-id="0de1d6d" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="0de1d6d">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-3ade5bf e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="3ade5bf" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
					<div class="e-con-inner">
				<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-d668002 elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor" data-id="d668002" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="text-editor.default">
				<div class="elementor-widget-container">
									<p class="isSelectedEnd">It looks as though the British weather has finally returned, so we are now beginning to wind down our first season at Goring Gap.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">I would like to take this opportunity to thank all our customers, staff, suppliers and supporters for making our first season such an incredible one. We were certainly blessed with some wonderful weather (and some amazing sun sets).</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">Looking back, we have achieved every objective we set ourselves for our first season.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">We successfully opened and operated a beach bar on a site with no mains running water, no mains electricity and no existing toilet facilities. We overcame each of those challenges by bringing in our own water, generating our own electricity and installing our own eco-friendly toilet facilities.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd"><strong>And the results speak for themselves.</strong></p><ul data-spread="false"><li>We welcomed and served more than 8,000 customers.</li><li>We achieved a Food Hygiene Rating of 5 — the highest possible.</li><li>Our premises licence operated without complaints.</li><li>The Police have confirmed there were no reports of any incidents on our site.</li><li>The predicted anti-social behaviour simply did not materialise.</li><li>There were no issues with light or noise pollution.</li><li>There was no increase in litter.</li><li>There were no accidents involving vehicles entering or leaving the site and, despite repeated concerns being raised before we opened, nobody was injured crossing the road to visit us.</li><li>More than 1,000 people came to Goring Gap to watch the eclipse, and the event passed safely and successfully.</li><li>We promoted Sussex and Worthing, employed local people, supported local businesses and suppliers and showcased Sussex-made products.</li><li>We created new facilities for residents and visitors.</li><li>We transformed our operation so that it now runs without fossil fuels and uses 100% green energy.</li><li>We actively embraced biodiversity and will continue improving the site environmentally. Even our lawnmowers are powered by green energy generated on site.</li><li>Most importantly — and thankfully — our defibrillator, the only readily available one on Goring Beach, was never needed.</li></ul><p class="isSelectedEnd">I think everyone involved can be extremely proud of what has been achieved in such a short space of time.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">Of course, not everyone supported what we were trying to do.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">A very small number of people chose to campaign strongly against the project, both online and, on occasions, outside our gates, where customers were approached as they left the site. We found some of that behaviour intrusive and unnecessary.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">Sadly, the debate also entered the political arena.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">The Green Party publicly opposed the project, including through local campaigning, leaflets, media comments and activity outside the site. Councillor Claire Hunt was among those who publicly expressed opposition. We were also disappointed when positive media coverage of the project itself became the subject of political discussion.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">People are absolutely entitled to disagree with what we are doing. That is democracy, and we respect that.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">But debate should be based on facts and conducted fairly.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">What has been opposed is a business that has taken a neglected piece of land — containing significant quantities of old stone, building rubble and other waste materials, which has NEVER been used for any agriculture — and brought it back into productive community use.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">No houses.<br />No flats.<br />No nursing homes.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">Just a community green space that thousands of people have now visited and enjoyed.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">Our commitment remains exactly the same: to support Worthing, promote Sussex, create local employment, work with local suppliers and continue improving the land environmentally.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">There are a few Councillors across Worthing who work hard to promote the town, its communities and its businesses. One example is Councillor Martin McCabe — whom we have never met and with whom we have no affiliation — whose enthusiasm for Worthing comes across clearly in the videos and content he produces. If every Councillor was as enthusiastic about our great town it would be a much better place.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">Positive promotion of our town matters.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd"><strong>Pebble Beach Café</strong></p><p class="isSelectedEnd">Our proposed design for Pebble Beach Café has also been incredibly well received.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">You can see our submission below, together with some of the recent press coverage <a href="https://www.facebook.com/story.php?story_fbid=1649222453878983&amp;id=100063737315639&amp;rdid=6wED9tVDSTN3xfXH#">here</a>.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">As many of you already know, we are passionate about both Worthing and Sussex.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">We want the very best for our town, for the people lucky enough to live here and for the many visitors who come to the coast and discover for themselves why we love living here so much.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">Our slogan sums that up rather well:</p><p class="isSelectedEnd"><strong>LOVE WORTHING — WORLD CHAMPIONS LIVE HERE ®</strong></p><p class="isSelectedEnd">And we even have our first postcard design!</p><p class="isSelectedEnd">Thank you again to everyone who visited us, supported us, worked with us and helped make our first season at Goring Gap such a memorable one.</p><p><strong>See you next season.</strong></p>								</div>
				</div>
					</div>
				</div>
		
</div>
<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-b390f2a e-con e-atomic-element e-flexbox-base e-default-div e-42f13fc " data-id="b390f2a" data-element_type="e-flexbox" data-e-type="e-flexbox" data-interaction-id="b390f2a">
    <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-519f958 e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="519f958" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container">
